
                           WELCOME TO BASE-ED!!
                           --------------------

 Written by Ray Akey (AKA Heavy Metal) using the SAS C development package.

The complete and MOST EFFICIENT (possibly the only?) subboard editing utility
    for CNet Amiga v2.27+ in which the "subboards" file is in SYSDATA:.

                                ** NOTE **
 Due to the painstaking time and effort that has been spent on this utility,
 I would appreciate anyone who really uses it often to register it with Metal
                                 Software.
                                **********

What is it?
-----------
 Simply the most useful subboard editing tool available.  It performs MANY
functions which will be FULLY explained in the text which follows.


Installation (we'll get to the good part first):
------------------------------------------------
  Copy Base-Ed to Pfiles:maintenance (or any other directory you may wish
to run it from.

  AO to your Pfiles:maintenance directory as a [C]Net P-File.
                            AND/OR
  Add to BBSMENU after the last command in "1; Maintenance Menu" using the
  following text:

  BED		|\#2Pfiles:maintenance/Base-Ed\

  ^^^may be changed to whatever command you wish to enter to call Base-Ed.


Operation:
----------
 Things can get really complex so I will go in steps to hopefully avoid
confusion:

First Prompt:  Simple.  Choose [U]DBase, [B]ase, or hit return to quit the
               program

View/Edit Screen: Looks very much like CNet's regular UD/Base (in fact, I
                  mistook it for exactly that one day :) )

                  Enter a Subboard number to view/edit the configuration
                  for that subboard.  Entering a SUBDIR number will prompt
                  you with text asking if you wish to edit entry/access
                  options for that Subdirectory.  If NO is selected, you are
                  taken into that Subdirectory.  If YES is selected, you
                  are taken to the Subboard edit/entry screen for that
                  Subdirectory.

                  All configuration variables are easily recognizable. So
                  if you know CNet well, this area will not be foreign to
                  you.

                  If changes are made in the "Entry Flags/Access" screen
                  for a subboard, upon exiting edit mode for that SubDir
                  you will be prompted whether or not you wish to make the
                  "CHILD" subboards (that is, subboards existing in that
                  subdirectory) default to the same settings.

                  If changes are made to a Subboard, only that Subboard's
                  settings are altered, and only if YOU make the changes,
                  unless you are in GLOBAL edit mode (we'll get to that soon).

                  Entering "/" will bring you to the parent directory,
                  and eventually, the ROOT directory.

                  A few more considerations are taken into account when
                  listing subs than are with the CNet message base processing,
                  in the following order of precedence:

                   1) if a Subboard is CLOSED then it is noted as such.
                   2) If a subboard is a (DIR) then it is noted as such.
                   3) If a subboard is an Echo sub, then it is noted (FIDO).
                   4) If a subboard is a UUCP sub, then it is noted (UUCP).
                   5) If a subboard is direct exchange, it is noted so.
                   6) If a subboard is restricted by GENDER, it is noted
                      accordingly.

