 **                When you are in the "[O]ther flags" edit mode, sometimes
 ***               you will get some weird text on the screen..  If this
*****              happens do not panic.  Simply enter the number for the
******             flag that has the weird text in it, and the weird text will
*****              dissapear and the "YES" or "NO" text will return.
 ***               REMEMBER: This IS A CNet bug. Some weird values got placed
 **                in those areas.  Whether it was from the current version of
 *  CNET BUG NOTE  CNet or an older version, BASE-ED FIXES THEM!

                 I'll leave the editing screen at this, the rest is pretty
                 well self-explanatory when you enter the program and begin
                 moving about.

GLOBAL CHANGES!:
----------------
 The single most powerful function a Message base utility can possess.

 Entering [G] in any list, takes you to the <GLOBAL MODEL> edit screen.
 Here, you can choose another subboard to model your configuration from
 (such as another subboard that has the settings, that you wish to use,
 already set.  Then, you MUST change at least 1 (one) variable before
 global changes will be made (this was intentionally done as a safeguard.
 This is a VERY POWERFUL function).

 NOTE: ALL variables of a the global model must be configured.  The settings
       which are active when you leave the "global model" screen (again, IF
       any changes are made) are the ones that will ultimately be made the
       defaults in 2 choices:

            1) GLOBALLY
            2) Only the current list (Subdirectory)

 So, if you are in a directory called "AMIGA SIG", you will be prompted
 whether you wish these changes to be made to all (GLOBAL) subboards and
 subdirectories in the Base or UDBase (whichever you are currently editing),
 or only the SUBBOARDS contained in "AMIGA SIG"  So make sure you are in
 the correct subboard if you only wish to make "Model" changes to a particular
 subdirectory.  the "Model" (or GLOBAL CHANGES) function was only provided
 to make life easier on new sysops or those sysops wishing to make a simple
 configuration sweep of all subboards, and then do the fine tuning afterwards.

 Note 2: When using the global changes option, if you choose "[C]urrent
         Directory" as the method of change, ONLY SUBBOARDS, and not
         subdirectories in the Current Directory, will be changed.
         This was done purposely due to the way subdirectories are handled
         in Base-Ed.  This may seem confusing while reading this doc, but once
         you have some hands on experience with Base-Ed, and explore it's
         capabilities, you'll catch on real quick. try it, nothing will be
         harmed.  Better yet, create a (DIR) and test Base-Ed on it, you won't
         be dissappointed!

